    clinical:
      "<b>Why this matters at the clinic and the post-mortem table.</b><br>" +
      "<b>Generalised, painless, symmetrical lymph node enlargement in a dog is lymphoma until proved otherwise</b>, and the proof takes one needle. Aspirate the node — not the mandibular node if you can avoid it, since it drains the mouth and is often reactive — and look for a monotonous population of large lymphoid cells. Check serum calcium at the same time, because hypercalcaemia points to T cell disease and a poorer outlook. Lymphoma is one of the few malignancies where chemotherapy reliably produces good quality remission, so the diagnosis is worth making promptly and discussing properly with the owner.<br><br>" +
      "In cattle, <b>enzootic bovine leukosis presents in as many ways as it has predilection sites</b>. Melena and chronic anaemia point to abomasal involvement; heart failure with jugular distension to right atrial infiltration; progressive hind limb weakness with a normal appetite to a spinal canal mass; and unilateral exophthalmos to retrobulbar tumour. When you meet any of these in an adult cow, palpate the superficial nodes and examine internal nodes on rectal. The herd message matters as much as the individual one: BLV spreads on needles, rectal sleeves and dehorning equipment, so <b>single-use needles and disinfected instruments are real disease control</b>, not just good manners.<br><br>" +
      "In poultry, distinguishing <b>Marek's disease from lymphoid leukosis</b> changes the advice you give the farm. Enlarged asymmetric sciatic nerves with bursal atrophy in birds from six weeks means Marek's, and the questions are about vaccination technique, hatchery hygiene and early exposure. Bursal tumours in birds over sixteen weeks with normal nerves means lymphoid leukosis, where the answer is eradication through breeding stock, since there is no vaccine.<br><br>" +
      "Finally, two points where the site tells you the prognosis. A pigmented mass in a <b>dog's mouth</b> is a serious malignancy needing staging and aggressive treatment, while a similar pigmented nodule on haired skin is often benign — the location, not the pigment, decides. And in a <b>grey horse</b>, examine the tail base and perineum at every routine visit; melanomas there are almost expected with age, and are far easier to manage while small than after years of unnoticed growth."
    ,

